#{[17592186226300 "Problems involving the symmetry of quadratic functions often test how well we can reason about a parabola without graphing it.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice using these patterns to draw logical conclusions about a quadratic function’s structure and behavior." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226301 "In this topic, we'll use our knowledge of the factor theorem to draw conclusions about unknown coefficients in quadratic equations." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226302 "Questions about quadratic functions in vertex form often focus on identifying or interpreting the maximum or minimum value of a parabola quickly.\n\nBelow, we’ll practice reading vertex form directly and using it to determine both the location and the value of a parabola’s highest or lowest point." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226303 "On the math section of the SAT exam, questions that describe physical relationships usually test our ability to translate written definitions into mathematical formulas.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice turning descriptions of physical phenomena into equations, using the given definitions to solve for unknown variables, and reinforcing formula reasoning through applied examples." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226304 "In this topic, we will determine unknown parameters in quadratic systems with zero or two solutions involving horizontal or sloped lines, as well as cases where both equations of the system contain parameters." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226305 "Solving quadratic equations with leading coefficients by factoring tests the ability to recognize when a quadratic can be factored quickly into linear factors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice factoring and solving quadratic equations with leading coefficients efficiently, developing the fluency needed to identify patterns and avoid time-consuming methods like the quadratic formula." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226306 "In this topic, we'll focus on recognizing and using structure to solve nonlinear systems of equations. We will practice substituting expressions with one or two variables and applying patterns such as the difference of squares to find solutions efficiently." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226307 "In this topic, we'll use our knowledge of polynomial graphs to determine their properties, such as the number of roots and valid outputs of polynomials over particular intervals. We'll also practice identifying the factored form of a polynomial from its graph." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226308 "In this topic, we'll use our knowledge of polynomial transformations to identify properties of transformed polynomials, including their roots, vertices, and tables of values." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230118 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may encounter questions that ask to add or subtract rational expressions, often with unlike denominators.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice turning sums and differences into a single simplified fraction by creating least common denominators, as well as distributing and canceling common factors." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230119 "Questions about expressing ratios as percentages test the ability to interpret part–whole relationships and convert fractions or ratios into percent form. In this topic, we’ll practice performing these conversions." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230120 "Questions about finding totals from percentages test whether we can reverse the usual percent-of-a-number process. Sometimes these problems appear in real-world contexts.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice setting up and solving equations to find the total from a known part and a percentage." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230121 "Questions about applying a percentage change test whether we can correctly use percentage increases or decreases in real-world and algebraic settings.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice applying percentage change to calculate new values, model practical scenarios, and strengthen our fluency with common exam-style percent word problems." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230122 "Problems about finding original values from the percentage change test our ability to work backward from a percentage increase or decrease. These problems often appear in real-world contexts.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice reversing percent increases and decreases to find original quantities." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230123 "In this topic, we'll practice determining percentage change in both abstract and real-world contexts." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230124 "Problems about successive percentage changes often involve chained growth or loss (such as multi-step discounts, compound price adjustments, or percentage comparisons).\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice working with multiple percentage changes." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230125 "In this topic, we'll practice calculating the median of datasets with odd and even numbers of values, and determining possible medians based on the data's distribution." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230126 "A key concept that often features on the math section of the SAT exam is understanding the relationship between the mean of a dataset and the sum of its values.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice expressing the mean as a fraction of the sum, combining means from different groups, and using sums to find unknown or constrained data values." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230127 "An important concept on the math section of the SAT exam is understanding how the mean and median relate to each other.\n\nHere, we will determine which measures can be calculated, find means and medians of consecutive integers, and use relationships between them to find missing values." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230128 "This topic provides practice in constructing and analyzing frequency tables. We will practice summarizing datasets, interpreting frequency values, determining ranges and extreme values, and working with joint frequency tables to compare categories." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230129 "On the math section of the SAT exam, an important concept is finding and comparing measures of center from frequency tables.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice calculating means and medians, comparing them when frequencies are unknown, and ordering measures of centrality using the given data." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230130 "This topic will strengthen our ability to calculate probabilities and their complements from datasets." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230131 "In this topic, we'll practice calculating and interpreting conditional probabilities using data from two-way frequency tables. We'll also find missing frequencies given a conditional probability." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230132 "In this topic, we will strengthen our skills in calculating conditional probabilities using data presented in two-way percentage tables or described in word problems." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230133 "In this topic, we will practice drawing conclusions based on the margin of error for population proportions and evaluate what they suggest about the accuracy and reliability of survey results." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230134 "Lines of best fit help us predict unknown values from data that follow a linear pattern.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice making these predictions." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230135 "Questions about best-fit lines often focus on interpreting their slope. In this topic, we’ll practice estimating and interpreting slopes of best-fit lines to understand trends." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230136 "On the math section of the SAT exam, questions about best-fit lines often contain graphs with axes that are not evenly scaled.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice reading and reasoning about such graphs." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230137 "Problems with time-series graphs usually test the ability to interpret changes over time.\n\nHere, we’ll practice reading these trends and reasoning through patterns." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230138 "Questions about the average rate of change often test our ability to measure how one quantity changes compared to another.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice estimating and calculating average rates of change from different types of plots." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230139 "In this topic, we'll practice analyzing data using box plots by identifying and estimating means, comparing distributions, and calculating differences in ranges." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230140 "This topic helps us practice in comparing datasets presented as dot plots. We will analyze their spreads, standard deviations, and relative variability." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230141 "An important skill needed for the SAT math section is recognizing how symmetry influences the mean and median of a dataset. Here, we will find missing values and compare measures of centrality using data, both from raw data and from tables." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230142 "This topic will help develop our skills in comparing measures of centrality using data presented in frequency tables." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230143 "Questions about unit conversions often appear in real-world contexts and require paying attention to conversion factors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice converting from smaller units of length to larger ones." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230144 "Questions about unit conversions for mass and volume test how well we can move between different measurement systems. In this topic, we’ll practice converting between these units." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230145 "On the math section of the SAT exam, problems about conversions between mixed units test whether we can convert rates by setting up corresponding factor-label chains.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice these conversions." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230146 "In this lesson, we’ll practice multi-step unit conversions." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230147 "Questions about mixed units with squares are common on the math section of the SAT exam. In this topic, we'll practice these types of conversions." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230148 "Multistep rate problems often combine two or more relationships. These questions test our ability to reason through proportional relationships logically rather than relying on a single formula.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice breaking down complex rate problems into smaller steps, identifying what each rate represents, and using consistent units to solve them." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230149 "Speed problems are among the most common applications of rate reasoning, often involving the relationship between distance, speed, and time.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice setting up and solving these problems systematically: translating words into equations, handling unit conversions, and mastering the logic behind distance–rate–time relationships." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230150 "On the math section of the SAT exam, ratio problems often require applying a given ratio to find an unknown quantity.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice setting up and solving proportions to find missing values." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230151 "In this topic, we’ll practice solving equations representing proportional relationships for a particular variable." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230152 "When solving ODEs numerically:\n\n- The solution is advanced in many steps.\n\n- Each step introduces a small error (LTE).\n\n- These errors propagate forward (contributing to GTE).\n\nStability describes how a numerical method responds to these small perturbations.\n\nA method is **stable** if small errors remain controlled as the computation proceeds.\nA method is **unstable** if small errors can grow rapidly, even when the true solution stays well-behaved.\n\nStability determines how local errors turn into global errors: a method may be accurate in a single step, but if it is unstable, the global error can grow without bound.\n\nOne way to study stability is to test a numerical method on the following **test equation**:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nThe exact solution of the test equation is $y(x) = e^{-\\lambda x},$ which satisfies $y(x) \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\infty.$\n\nWe say that a numerical method with update formula \n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y\n$$\n\n is **stable** for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation, the numerical solution also satisfies\n\n$$\ny_n \\to 0 \\:\\text{ as }\\: n \\to \\infty.\n$$\n\nTo see how this works in practice, let's analyze the stability of Euler's method." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230153 "We know how to apply the implicit Euler method to initial value problems\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthat are linear in $y,$ that is, where $f(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x).$\nHowever, in general, $f$ can be a nasty, nonlinear function in $y.$ For example, consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - xy^4,\\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCan we apply the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 0.5$ to this IVP to approximate the value of $y(0.5)?$\n\nYes, we can! However, doing so requires defining a function $g$ to which the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a solution of the equation $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$ Then, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nTo see how, let's start by applying the implicit Euler method as we normally would. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the update formula $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4} \\\\ 0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with terms such as $0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^4$ and $y_\\text{new},$ there’s no simple algebraic rearrangement, and solving exactly is impractical.\n\nInstead, let's define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} - y - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that, at any step, $y$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function: $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ Therefore, to find $y_\\text{new},$ we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new}=0+0.5=0.5.$ So, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5\\cdot 0.5\\cdot ({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-0.5\\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230154 "Problems involving fractions of fractions may also appear on the math section of the SAT exam to test the ability to simplify layered rational expressions.\n\nHere, we’ll practice reducing complex fractional expressions step by step, focusing on recognizing structure, factoring common terms, and rewriting them as simple rational forms." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230155 "Problems involving simplifying exponential expressions often require applying the laws of exponents. In this topic, we’ll practice factoring, combining, and simplifying exponential terms using these laws." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230156 "Questions involving exponential expressions in conjunction with the distributive law could appear on the math section of the SAT exam.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice applying the distributive property with expressions containing integer and fractional exponents." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230157 "In this topic, we will find lower and upper bounds for the mean, median, and range from frequency tables or histograms, and determine the largest or smallest possible differences between related statistics." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230158 "In this topic, we'll focus on finding missing observations based on the mean, median, or mode of a dataset." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230159 "A typical question that often crops up on the SAT math section involves understanding how modifying data affects statistical measures.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice determining how adding, subtracting, or adjusting values changes statistics such as the mean, median, range, and standard deviation." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230160 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may be asked to demonstrate how changes to data affect the line of best fit.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice recognizing these effects so we can adjust the corresponding equations of the lines." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230161 "Questions about residuals usually test the ability to compare an actual point to the corresponding point on the line of best fit.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying overestimates and underestimates, including identifying data points that struggle to fit the general trend." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230162 "In this topic, we’ll practice spotting whether a situation describes steady, additive change (linear) or rapid, multiplicative growth or decay (exponential)." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230163 "Questions about percentages represented by variables test our ability to form and solve equations involving unknown percentages.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice setting up and solving equations that connect parts, wholes, and percentages." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230164 "In this lesson, we’ll practice solving multistep percentage problems that include successive markups and discounts, reversed percentage relationships, and finding original prices or costs." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230165 "Questions about applying the successive percentage changes test our ability to handle multiple percent increases or decreases in sequence. These problems often appear in real-world scenarios.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice combining percentage changes." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230166 "Ratio questions appear in many settings, such as rates with variables, geometry, and word problems.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice turning real-world and algebraic descriptions into proportions, then solving them." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150,$ and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40%\\times 150 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 150 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60.$\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25%\\times 60 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 60 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15.$" #edb/ref 13194139535252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230168 "In previous lessons, we learned how to apply percentage increases and decreases. We can refer to percentage increases and decreases collectively as **percentage change.** In this lesson, we'll learn how to find what percentage change is applied to a number to get another number.\n\nTo set the stage, let's first recall how to calculate a percentage increase in a retail situation.\n\nSuppose that a fast-food meal in a particular restaurant costs $\\[math]10$. The manager then decides to increase the price by $20\\%$. What is the new price of the meal?\n\nRecall that *increasing* the price of the meal by $20\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 20\\% = 120\\%\n$$\n\nThus, the new price is $120\\%$ of the old price.\n\nExpressing $120\\%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n120\\% = \\dfrac{120}{100} = 1.2\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\[/math]10 \\times 1.2 = \\[math]12\n$$\n\nSo, the price of the meal after the increase is $\\[/math]12$." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230169 "In real-world applications, it's common to estimate values. However, estimation often leads to **error**, which is the difference between a quantity's estimated and actual values.\n\nFor example, if a real estate agent estimates that a house is worth $\\[math]180\\,000$ but its actual market value is $\\[/math]150\\,000$, then the total error in the agent's estimate is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{error} = \\[math]180\\,000 - \\[/math]150\\,000 = \\[math]30\\,000\n$$\n\nIs this an acceptable amount of error?\n\nErrors by themselves are often difficult to interpret. For example, an error of $1$ centimeter is acceptable when designing a parking lot, but such an error could have serious consequences when performing medical procedures.\n\nTo resolve this, it's often more helpful to compute the **percentage error**. The formula for the percentage error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNotice that this is virtually identical to the formula for percentage change. The only difference is the terminology used.\n\nLet's go back to our real estate question. The estimated value is $\\[/math]180\\,000$, and the exact value is $\\[math]150\\,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage error &= \\frac{\\mid estimate - exact value\\begin{vmatrix}}{exact value} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 180000 - 150000 \\mid}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 30000\\end{vmatrix}}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{30000}{150000} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{15} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 20%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percentage error in the agent's estimation is $20\\%$, which is relatively high." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230170 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage increase to a number to get a new, larger number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage increase and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?\n\nIncreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 35\\% = 135\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the increased value is $\\[/math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]300$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $35\\%$ increase to $\\[/math]300{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\[math]300 \\cdot (100% + 35%) &= \\[/math]300 \\cdot 135% \\\\\n&= \\[math]300 \\cdot 1.35 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]405✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 "When percentages are applied in succession, it is useful to represent their combined effect as a single percentage of the original amount. \n\nThis means that, instead of applying one percentage and then another, we can multiply their decimal multipliers to find an overall multiplier (or percentage) for the total change. \n\nFor example, suppose that a city’s library membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Of these members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. What percentage of last year’s members borrowed digital books?\n\nFirst, let $x$ be the number of members in the previous year.\n\nWe're told that membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Thus, the new number of members is \n\n$$\n1.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. So, the number of members who borrowed digital books is\n\n$$\n0.4(1.5x) = 0.6x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.6x$ represents $60\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $60\\%$ of last year’s members borrowed digital books." #edb/ref 13194139535252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230172 "In a previous lesson, we spent some time learning how to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This is known as finding a *percentage increase.*\n\nWe can apply similar reasoning to find a number that is a certain percentage *less than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a **percentage decrease.**\n\nTo apply a percentage decrease, we do the following:\n\n1\\. Subtract the decrease from $100\\%.$\n\n2\\. Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n\n3\\. Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nLet's take a look at a worked example." #edb/ref 13194139535463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230173 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage decrease to a number to get a new, smaller number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage decrease and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $5\\%$ decrease, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]228$. What was the original fee?\n\nDecreasing a number by $5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 5\\% = 95\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n95\\%=\\frac{95}{100}=0.95\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $5\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.95x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the decreased value is $\\[/math]228$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.95x &= 228 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{228}{0.95} \\\\\nx &= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]240$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $5\\%$ decrease to $\\[/math]240{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\[math]240 \\cdot (100% - 5%) &= \\[/math]240 \\cdot 95% \\\\\n&= \\[math]240 \\cdot 0.95 \\\\\n&= \\[/math]228✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230174 "Now, let's consider the reverse problem. Suppose we know that the original price of the meal was $\\[math]10$ and the new price is $\\[/math]12$. We know that the manager applied a $20\\%$ increase, but how can we determine this percentage change from the initial and final prices alone?\n\nTo compute a percentage change, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This formula defines percentage change as the ratio of the absolute change in a quantity (the numerator) relative to its original value (the denominator), expressed as a percentage.\n- Note the presence of the absolute value in the numerator. This allows us to apply it to both percentage increases and percentage decreases, as percentage change should always be positive.\n\nNow, in our situation, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = \\[math]12$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = \\[/math]10$\n\nSo, to compute the percentage change, we use the given formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npercentage change &= \\frac{\\mid new value - original value\\begin{vmatrix}}{original value} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 12 - 10 \\mid}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2\\end{vmatrix}}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{10} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 100% \\\\\n&= 20%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $20\\%$, exactly as we saw earlier." #edb/ref 13194139533384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230175 "We wish to construct the general solution to the first-order linear inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nThere are several ways to find the general solution to a first-order inhomogeneous ODE. The method we'll discuss here is a three-step process that involves finding the sum of the so-called complementary and particular solutions. \n\nThe three steps are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**) $y_c(x)$ that satisfies the associated homogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. This is a single function that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution: \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x).\n$$\n\nAs we'll see, this method generalizes to higher-order linear ODEs, so it's well worth understanding how it works for first-order ODEs.\n\nLet's begin by practicing finding the complementary solution to an equation." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230176 "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with **constant coefficients**\n\n$$\na y' + b y = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants ($a \\neq 0$).\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **sinusoidal forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is a sine or cosine function, or a combination of both. It is typically written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = C \\cos(\\omega x) + D \\sin(\\omega x)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ and $D$ are constants (not both zero).\n\nNow, if we choose only a sine or only a cosine as a trial function for the particular solution, differentiation will introduce the other sinusoidal term, since the derivative of a sinusoid is another sinusoid. Substituting such a trial solution into the ODE therefore produces both sine and cosine terms.\n\nTo ensure that this combination can match the forcing term $f(x),$ the trial guess *must* include both sine and cosine terms of the same frequency $\\omega,$ even when $f(x)$ itself contains only one of them.\n\nTherefore, our trial guess for the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(\\omega x) + \\beta \\sin(\\omega x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with sinusoidal forcing. Let's look at a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230177 "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with constant coefficients given by\n\n$$\nay' + by = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **exponential forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = Ke^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a constant. \n\nProvided that the forcing function $e^{kx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, our trial guess for the particular solution is an exponential function with the same exponent. That is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha\\,e^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with **exponential forcing**. Let's look at a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230178 "So far, our guesses for the particular solution have worked because the forcing term did not already appear in the complementary solution.\n\nSometimes, however, the right-hand side of the differential equation has the same form as a term that appears in the complementary solution $y_c(x).$ When this happens, our usual guess for $y_p(x)$ fails because it duplicates part of the complementary solution and cannot produce a new solution.\n\nIn this situation, we must modify our guess by *multiplying it by $x.$* Specifically, if the forcing term has the form $K e^{kx}$ and the complementary solution contains a term like $Ae^{kx},$ then instead of guessing\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{kx},\n$$\n\nwe try\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{x} e^{kx}.\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying by $x$ creates a new function that is *linearly independent* of the complementary solution. As a result, our guess no longer overlaps with $y_c(x)$ and can serve as a valid particular solution. We'll discuss linear independence of functions in more detail in future lessons.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice using a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230179 "We've discussed how to find the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ of a first-order inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nWe will now turn our attention to finding a **particular solution.**\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ of a linear ODE captures the part of the solution that arises directly from the forcing term $f(x).$\nIn other words, if we substitute $y_p(x)$ into the left-hand side of the equation, it should produce the forcing function $f(x).$\n\nFor equations with constant coefficients, we can often find $y_p(x)$ by looking at the type of function appearing on the right-hand side and then guessing a form that matches it. This is sometimes called **the method of undetermined coefficients.**\n\nFor example, if the forcing function $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n,$ we guess a particular solution that is also a polynomial of degree $n.$  After making this guess, we substitute it into the differential equation and solve for the unknown constants.\n\nNext, we'll work through a concrete example where $f(x)$ is a linear polynomial." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230180 "Now that we've practiced finding both the complementary and particular solutions, let's put them together to find the **general solution**.\n\nRecall that the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ comes from solving the associated homogeneous equation. It contains an arbitrary constant, so it represents all homogeneous solutions.\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ is one specific function that satisfies the full inhomogeneous equation.\n\nTo find the general solution, we add these two pieces:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x).\n$$\n\nThe particular solution already satisfies the full equation.\nAdding any complementary solution preserves this property while introducing the arbitrary constant needed to match different initial conditions.\n\nIn this way, the formula above describes *every possible solution* of a linear inhomogeneous equation.\n\nLet's consider an example." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230181 "We will now check the stability of Euler's method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n, y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\lambda {y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)((1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's define $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x.$ Then, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$ The function $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is called the **stability function** of Euler's method.\n\nExpanding this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1 \\\\ -1<1-\\lambda Δx & <1 \\\\ -2<-\\lambda Δx & <0 \\\\ 0<\\lambda Δx & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of Euler's method for the cases $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ and $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ with $\\lambda > 0$ fixed.\n\n![](images/30/30dc27c192c43aa4fb57ed79f8412b85d106e662ce0416f814621d482d2466ea.png)\n\nNotice that, for $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2,$ the numerical values converge to $0$ (stable), whereas for $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ the values oscillate between $-1$ and $1$ and do not converge to $0$ (unstable).\n\nFor values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 2,$ the numerical solution would grow in magnitude at each step, leading to rapidly diverging values." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230182 "In general, when a numerical method\n$y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$\nis applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nwe can write the update in the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the **stability function** of the method.\n\nFor stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a geometric sequence, this decays to $0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$\n\nThe set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the **stability region** of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called **absolutely stable**.\n\nWe've seen that Euler's method is *not* absolutely stable, since its stability region is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$ But what about the implicit Euler method? Let's find out." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230183 "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ (where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $|R(\\lambda \\Delta x)| < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230184 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}$?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t).$ Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x,$ but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-tx}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}({e}^{-tx})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-x{e}^{-tx}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant." #edb/ref 13194139534774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230185 "Now, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\lambda {y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+\\lambda Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThe method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.$\n\nSince $1 + \\lambda\\Delta x > 1$ for any positive step size $\\Delta x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nfor all $\\Delta x > 0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable!\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.$ \nWe saw before that the explicit method oscillates between the values $-1$ and $1$ and does not converge to $0.$\n\nConversely, the numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to $0$ quite quickly for the same step size!\n\n![](images/08/083b5c0193a2a35648434cae4dcf1820fd47c86202c13a7e7e2e078d45d52bdb.png)\n\nImplicit methods tend to be more numerically stable than explicit methods." #edb/ref 13194139534913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230186 "We know how to apply the trapezoidal method to initial value problems that are linear in $y.$ Can we apply it to more complex initial value problems, such as\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2 - y^4, \\qquad y(0)=1?\n$$\n\nYes, we can. To do so, we define a function $g$ such that the next $y$-value, $y_\\text{new},$ satisfies the equation $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$ Then, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's apply the trapezoidal method to the IVP above with step size $\\Delta x = 0.5.$\n\nWe begin as usual. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the update formula for $\\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, there is no simple algebraic rearrangement to isolate $y_\\text{new},$ so solving exactly is impractical.\nInstead, we define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that $x,$ $y,$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants at each step, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function: $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ Therefore, to find $y_\\text{new},$ we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 0.5 = 0.5.$ So, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25\\cdot (1)^{4}-1-0.5\\cdot (0)^{2}-0.5\\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230187 "Consider a body of mass $m$ falling vertically, influenced only by gravity $g$ and drag $D.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ecacfcd964cb91f1cc35c4b9abe8e0969b85c02703eaf34e26612a9a624fdcc.png)\n\nAt higher speeds, or for larger objects, drag is often approximately proportional to the square of the body’s velocity. This model is called **quadratic drag**.\n\nAssume that both $m$ and $g$ are constant, and choose the downward direction as the positive direction.\n\nTwo forces act on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity is the weight, which equals $mg.$\n- The drag force is $$D=-kv^2,$$ where $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality (this force is negative because it acts upward, opposite the motion).\n\nHence, the net force on the body is $$F = mg - kv^2.$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the body, we get $$\\begin{aligned}F & =mg-k{v}^{2} \\\\ ma & =mg-k{v}^{2} \\\\ m\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} & =mg-k{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, writing in standard form, we get $$m\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + kv^2 = mg \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{k}{m}v^2 = g}.$$" #edb/ref 13194139533460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230188 "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving homogeneous systems of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix.\n\nFor example, consider the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/f9/f94140f8c9fe736ca7c574547658856e85d938eb08064046716be8eb1a2748de.svg)\nwhere ![](images/26/26dcf2c367c915460d09f4f38b40e4f792a06b6ab44291b4739a6935529daf89.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/90/9000e03ddf617a1e3c4e0eed5da2b846d62e3d68588b601d90643783960d17df.svg)\n\nRecall that given the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nSince we have already seen how to compute the standard fundamental matrix, we will not repeat the calculation here. So, computing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/11/11e0da79656b7f8e2c84c6c54e6dd6b54cf53cbb8e746d44dfb13329c3d2ed8f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n**Note:** Recall that for the general solution of the ordinary differential equation $x'(t)=ax(t),$ we have \n\n$$\nx'(t)=ax(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nx(t) = c \\, e^{at}, \n\\quad\nc \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nNow, for the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186230189 "A differential equation is a **d'Alembert differential equation** if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f(y') = y',$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny = x(y')^2 + \\dfrac{1}{y'}\n$$\n\nis a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = (y')^2, \\qquad g(y') = \\dfrac{1}{y'}.\n$$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{y' - x}\n$$\n\nis *not* a d'Alembert differential equation because the term $\\sin x$ prevents it from being written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y').$\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230190 "Now, consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-2\\right) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2 - 3\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSuppose we apply the substitution $y'(x) = t.$ Using the techniques shown earlier in the lesson (differentiating with respect to $x$), we can reduce this to the following first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} - \\dfrac12 x = 2t-\\dfrac32.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of integrating factors, we compute $I(t)$ based on the coefficient $P(t) = -\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\nI(t) = e^{\\int -1/2\\,\\textrm dt} = e^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ converts the left-hand side into an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-t/2} & =(2t-\\frac{3}{2}){e}^{-t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/2}) & =(2t-\\frac{3}{2}){e}^{-t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-t/2} & =\\int (2t-\\frac{3}{2}){e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =2\\int t{e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts for the first integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-t/2} & =2(-2t-4){e}^{-t/2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-2){e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =(-4t-8){e}^{-t/2}+3{e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =(-4t-5){e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x(t) & =-4t-5+C{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to construct the function $y,$ we simply substitute $y' = t$ and $x = x(t)$ into our original equation. Note that the solution $y(t)$ is dependent on the parameter $t.$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230191 "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving *inhomogeneous systems* of ODEs as well. Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, and suppose we are given an initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\n\nOur goal is to derive the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system.\n\nFirst, we eliminate the *inhomogeneous term* by a change of variables. We define\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand differentiate it using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =\\cancel{-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+\\cancel{A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate both sides from $t_0$ to $t$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to the initial variable $\\mathbf{x}(t).$ To do that we multiply both sides by $e^{A(t-t_0)}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our IVP is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere the first term represents the solution of the corresponding *homogeneous system*, and the second term gives a particular solution of our *inhomogeneous system*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 13194139535224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230192 "Before proceeding with the next example, let's recall some important properties of matrix exponentials:\n\n1\\. *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n  $$\n  (e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n  $$\n\n3\\. *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n  $$\n  e^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n  $$\n\n4\\. *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n  $$\n\n  **Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute.\n\nLet's see how these properties can be used in practice." #edb/ref 13194139534955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230193 "Now, let's find the solution to the initial value problem below\n![](images/31/3111f065d441881e050417b345b34947509f175f00ebd5402963e432d013348e.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/5c/5c4ed0a2e9db0979e4fc0342c2e4db7c23c60cf58293a496fa661ff1c03fccde.svg)\nwhere ![](images/71/7164659247c5290544655b05943d7d998f67cb1cc3b4cc45bab0902e8059bc75.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/e3/e3f94e8ce2cc2063d469fbac2af87232cc130f788243c1507bfe9972dd4b04c1.svg)\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nEvaluating both sides of $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\mathbf{c}$ at $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c \\\\ x(0) & ={e}^{A(0)}\\,c \\\\ x(0) & =I\\,c \\\\ c & =x(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to our initial value problem is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0).\n$$\n\nComputing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/fc/fc4e1ba16be420fbe5e53e2dbb4ee6da96c7097bbe4e117315407ec4dc65365f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(3-2t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -2{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535474 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186230194 "Next, let's find the solution to the initial value problem \n![](images/93/93f9c58b8a9cea64ba97f43f2831336ea8d38a6fd6165211a54d2822415c1497.svg)\ngiven that ![](images/ee/ee25228bf71d1b7d9d0688af06a7f1335c252310127b403ed0ed37f7fe277c07.svg) where ![](images/3a/3a3ac9326741c0e2390a8e2f079808f0b432b740149ef4b034ffa7568dc33848.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/2b/2b81cf358ffb3df49f327b63138640ac452a249f989353b521be34d531aeffd9.svg)\nwhere ![](images/11/1110f0bdbe784b21683bfb9610ca9c04e5a45755954d49a15124e530feef2002.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/47/47d8a0214e5ccdf6625a330d23003dec11cbc3a4f35cb2992df29a688963e4b2.svg)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution is $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}.$ We can determine the constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$ using the initial condition at time $t=t_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c} \\implies \\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0),\n$$\n\nsince the inverse of $e^{At_0}$ is $e^{-At_0}.$ Plugging this back into our general solution gives the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our specific problem (with $t_0=1$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535474 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186230195 "Recall that the *terminal velocity* is the constant speed a falling object approaches when the upward resistive force (such as drag) becomes large enough to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\n\nIf drag is proportional to the square of velocity, with a constant of proportionality $k > 0,$ this balance occurs when $$mg - k(v_\\text{term})^2 = 0.$$\n\nSolving for the terminal velocity, we get $$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{v_\\text{term} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{mg}{k}}}.$$\n\nLet's determine a terminal velocity in the next example." #edb/ref 13194139533460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230196 "Suppose a body of mass $125\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=49\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nAssuming the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2,$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag, let's find the equation for the velocity $v(t).$\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight $$w = mg = 125g,$$ and the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction): $$D = -kv^2 = -49v^2$$ Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is $$F = w + D = 125g - 49v^2.$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 125g-49{v}^{2} & =125\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.392{v}^{2} \\\\ & =g-0.04g{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.04{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.2v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.2}\\text{artanh}(0.2v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\text{artanh}(0.2v) & =\\frac{1}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.2v & =\\operatorname{tanh}(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C),\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 0,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(\\frac{1}{5}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 0 & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$v(t) = 5\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{5} g t\\right)$$" #edb/ref 13194139533460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230197 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\qquad t \\in [c,d].\n$$\n\nThen, *differentiation under the integral sign* applies, meaning\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\int_{a}^b \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x,\n$$\n\nprovided the following conditions are met:\n\n- $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d],$ and\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (2x+t^3)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x, \\qquad t \\in [0,5].\n$$\n\nWe may differentiate under the integral sign provided that the function $f(x,t) = (2x+t^3)^2$ fits the necessary criteria. Let's check.\n\nFix $t_0 \\in (0,5)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,5],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\n- Notice that $f(x,t) = (2x+t^3)^2$ is continuous on $R.$\n\n- Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = 6(2x+t^3)t^2,\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$\n\nThus, the operation is valid. We obtain\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} 6(2x+t^3)t^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230198 "Recall that a linear first-order homogeneous differential equation in the variable $y = y(x)$ takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, the equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6xy = 0\n$$\n\nis a linear first-order homogeneous ODE with $a(x) = 1$ and $b(x) = 6x.$ Notice that the right-hand side is identical to zero.\n\nA linear first-order *inhomogeneous* differential equation is similar, except the right-hand side is *not* identical to zero. So, a linear first-order inhomogeneous differential equation can be written as\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is often called the **forcing function.** We say that a first-order ODE has **polynomial forcing** if $f(x)$ is a polynomial.\n\nFor example, the equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6xy = 2x\n$$\n\nis a linear first-order inhomogeneous ODE with polynomial forcing because the right-hand side, $f(x) = 2x,$ is a nonzero polynomial.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to find general solutions to linear first-order ODEs with polynomial forcing." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230199 "Consider the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\na(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove that the sum of the complementary and particular solutions gives a general solution to the equation. \n\nFirst, we note that, by definition, the complementary solution $y_c$ satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_c + b(x)y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nThis can be solved by separating the variables, and the solution is given by (for $a(x)\\neq 0$)\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\exp\\left(-\\int \\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}\\,\\textrm d x\\right).\n$$\n\nBy definition, the particular solution satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe claim that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the most general solution to the equation. \n\n- First, we show that $y_c + y_p$ satisfies the equation. Substituting this into the left-hand side of the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y & =a(x)({y}_{c}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)({y}_{c}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & ={{[a(x){y}_{c}^{'}+b(x){y}_{c}]}_{⏟}}_{0}+[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}] \\\\  & =a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, suppose that $y$ is *any* solution to the equation, and $y_p$ is a known, particular solution. Substituting $y - y_p$ into the left-hand side of the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x)(y-{y}_{p})^{'}+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) & =a(x)({y}^{'}-{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =[a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y]-[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is a solution, we have $a(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x)$, and since $y_p$ is a particular solution, we have $a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left[a(x)y' + b(x)y\\right] - \\left[a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p\\right]\n= f(x) - f(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, $y - y_p$ satisfies the homogeneous equation, and hence $y - y_p = y_c$. This implies that any solution $y$ is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p.$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230200 "Consider an object shot straight up from the surface of the Earth, assuming the only force acting on the object is gravity (ignore air resistance and any thrust). How do we model its velocity?\n\n![](images/34/347959343d843e1a9d097779aa43ca24629638edeb79e8af5e3913211ebc97f6.png)\n\nAccording to Newton's law of gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward Earth is $$F_\\text{in} = G\\dfrac{mM}{r^2},$$ where\n\n- $G$ is the gravitational constant,\n- $m$ is the mass of the object,\n- $M$ is the mass of the Earth, and\n- $r=r(t)$ is the distance between their centers of mass.\n\nAt the initial position, when the object is on the surface of the Earth, $r$ equals the radius of the planet $R.$\n\nThen, taking the outward direction as positive and applying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have $$\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}$$ In other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, $$a=\\dfrac{k}{r^2},$$ with constant of proportionality $k=-GM<0.$\n\nTo find $k,$ we use the conditions at the Earth's surface. When $r=R$ (Earth's radius), the acceleration is $a=-g$ (where $g$ is standard gravity). Substituting these values: $$-g = \\dfrac{k}{R^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = -gR^2.$$ Substituting $k$ back into our equation for acceleration, we obtain $$a = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}.$$\n\nNow, since $a=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we can express acceleration as a derivative of velocity with respect to distance $r$ as follows (treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory): $$\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ & =\\frac{dv}{dr}\\cdot \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\ & =\\frac{dv}{dr}\\cdot v \\\\ & =v\\,\\frac{dv}{dr}.\\end{aligned}$$ Therefore, substituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity: $$v\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}.$$" #edb/ref 13194139533460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230201 "Now, let's solve the separable differential equation for the velocity: $$v\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}$$\n\nUsing separation of variables, we have $$\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nRecall that when the object is at the surface of Earth, $r=R.$ Let $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0,$ we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity is $$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{v^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR}.$$" #edb/ref 13194139533460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230202 "Recall that the equation for the velocity in our model is $$v^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR.$$\n\nNow, for an object to *escape* the gravitational pull of the Earth and never go backward, we require two things:\n\n- a positive initial velocity, and\n- the velocity to never be zero.\n\nThe minimum required initial velocity that allows an object to escape from the surface of the Earth is called the **escape velocity**.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that the term $\\dfrac{2gR^2}{r}$ decreases as $r$ increases. This means the velocity is lowest when $r \\to \\infty,$ where this term approaches zero.\n\nTherefore, to ensure the velocity never vanishes, the remaining terms must be non-negative: $$\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus, the minimum velocity of an object to escape the surface of Earth is $$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{v_e = \\sqrt{2gR}}.$$\n\n**Note:** On the other hand, if $v_0^2 - 2gR < 0,$ then there will be a critical value of $r$ for which the velocity becomes zero. Hence, the object would stop, the velocity would switch direction, and the object would free-fall, returning to Earth." #edb/ref 13194139533460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230203 "So far, we've used differential equations to model quantities in circuits containing resistors and inductors. Now, we'll focus on circuits containing resistors and capacitors.\n\nA **capacitor** is a component of an electrical circuit that opposes *changes* in voltage across it by storing energy in an electric field.\n\n![](images/37/3729fc4786e8677a1a7bf20aa013d6c6943c6d69c73adc63dd743c3918e65db4.png)\n\nThe capacitance $C$ (measured in farads) of a capacitor determines how much charge $q$ (measured in coulombs) the capacitor stores for a given voltage, $V_C,$ according to the equation \n\n$$\nq = CV_C.\n$$\n\nAn **RC circuit** is an electrical circuit that contains both a resistor and a capacitor, most commonly in series with a voltage source. An example, with current $I(t),$ is shown below, consisting of a resistance $R,$ in ohms, a capacitance $C,$ in farads, and a source electromotive force (emf) $E,$ in volts.\n\n![](images/fe/feef8e5b715b6b0442dc27a65886d611b27d4d912842f992790e6309a78afda4.png)\n\nLet $q(t)$ denote the charge on the capacitor, measured in coulombs, as a function of time $t.$ Can we find a differential equation that best describes the charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor?\n\nFirst, note that since current is the rate at which electric charge flows, the circuit's current is the derivative of charge with respect to time: \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nNow, recall Kirchhoff's Voltage law, which states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops around the loop.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The resistor *opposes the flow of current*, hence producing a voltage drop $V_R$ proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R{:}$\n\n$$\nV_R = RI = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\n- The capacitor *opposes changes in voltage*, hence producing a voltage drop $V_C$ related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ So, \n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's Voltage Law, the source voltage $E$ is the sum of the voltage drops: $E = V_R + V_C.$\n\nAs a result, the basic equation governing the amount of charge on the capacitor $q$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\nE = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{q}{C}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{RC}q = \\dfrac{E}{R}.\n$$\n\nNote that since $I(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ solving for $q(t)$ also tells us the current in the circuit." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230204 "Consider a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\sin(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitance of $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}.$ Let's find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 40\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 40I = 40\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\sin(2t) = 40\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sin(2t).\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230205 "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative \n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $\\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230206 "In a first-order linear differential equation, the solution naturally splits into a transient term that decays over time and a steady-state term that remains as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nIn an RC circuit with a constant electromotive force (EMF), this steady-state value corresponds to the charge the capacitor approaches as $t \\to \\infty,$ regardless of the initial charge. Physically, this represents the maximum charge the capacitor can store under a constant applied voltage.\n\nTo demonstrate how to identify the steady-state charge directly from the general solution of an RC circuit model, suppose the charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 12q = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} +12q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 12\\alpha = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac{7}{120}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{7}{120}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-12t} + \\dfrac{7}{120}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = Ae^{-12t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac{7}{120}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge (the charge the capacitor approaches as time increases) is ${\\dfrac{7}{120}}$ coulombs." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230207 "In a first-order linear RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient charge, which decays over time, and a steady-state charge, which oscillates with the same frequency as the input.\n\nAs time increases, the transient charge vanishes, leaving a steady-state charge that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state charge in the RC circuit below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc700238d93fd6529c7113cd1a650e8c03d796e606afd4b655123cd04ed11cc7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 50\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 50I = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t) = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q = \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cos(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=\\frac{2}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{6}{125}$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-3t} + \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t) \\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{125}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{8}{125}\\right)^2} =0.08\\,\\textrm{coulombs}.\n$$\n\nThis amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the oscillating steady-state charge on the capacitor." #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186230208 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ the equation can be transformed to a *first-order linear equation* of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t),\n$$\n\nwhich we already know how to solve.\n\nThis can be done in three steps. Let's see a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
